+# NG-9 Narrowed: Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 kept search out of scope for every entity but the owner, and the veterinarian was named among them. The row was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, so whether it was deliberately declined stayed open.
+
+The owner has now decided (2026-08-23). The decision distinguishes two things the single row conflated: a free-text search over veterinarians, and a filter that narrows the already-published directory to one specialty it already displays. The first stays out; the second is wanted.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole.** Rejected: it would keep the specialty filter out on a rationale ("repeating paged prefix search adds surface, not understanding") that does not apply — filtering a cached collection by an attribute already on the page is not a search and teaches a distinct pattern.
+2. **Drop NG-9.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian, pet, and visit search remain deliberately out; the row still carries them.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen). Keep free-text search for pet, visit, and veterinarian out; admit an attribute filter over the veterinarian directory.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian stays out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already shows is in scope, realized by REQ-VET-001 (the paged HTML surface) and REQ-VET-003 (the machine-readable surface). A filter over a displayed attribute is not a search and carries none of the free-text search surface the row declines.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 admits directory filtering; free-text search remains declined for pet, visit, and veterinarian.
+- The machine-readable `/vets` surface, withdrawn as REQ-VET-002, is reinstated as a supported surface under REQ-VET-003. The id REQ-VET-002 stays retired and is not reused.
+- A visible page control (form, dropdown) for the filter is not part of this decision; the filter is a URL contract only, and a control may come as a later request.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [Contracts — specialty-filter paragraph](../system-design.md#contracts) — the surface realizing REQ-VET-003.
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-001) — REQ-VET-001 and REQ-VET-003, the filter's two surfaces.
